TypeScript works well, and it's a better fit for your keys than Python. The neatlogs TypeScript SDK officially supports the **Vercel AI SDK**, so all three of your keys work with it.

## neatlogs TypeScript SDK (`npm install neatlogs`)

Install it with `npm install neatlogs@latest`. It needs Node 18 or newer, and you have Node 26. TypeScript has **no auto-instrumentation**, so you wrap each client or framework explicitly:

| What you build with | neatlogs wrapper |
|---|---|
| **Vercel AI SDK** (`ai` v6/v7) ⭐ | `wrapAISDK(ai)` |
| OpenAI SDK (also works for DeepSeek or OpenRouter via `baseURL`) | `wrapOpenAI(client)` |
| `@openrouter/agent` | `wrapOpenRouterAgent(client)` |
| Claude Agent SDK | `wrapClaudeAgentSDK(sdk)` |
| OpenAI Agents SDK | `openaiAgentsProcessor()` |
| Mastra | `wrapMastra(entity)` |
| LangChain / LangGraph | `langchainHandler()` |
| Your own functions | `span({ kind: 'AGENT' \| 'TOOL' \| 'WORKFLOW' }, fn)` |

## Recommended setup: Vercel AI SDK plus your key

```bash
npm install neatlogs@latest ai zod
npm install @openrouter/ai-sdk-provider   # if using OpenRouter
# or: npm install @ai-sdk/deepseek        # if using DeepSeek
# Vercel AI Gateway key: nothing extra; the `ai` package uses it natively
```

```ts
import * as ai from 'ai';
import { init, wrapAISDK, span, flush, shutdown } from 'neatlogs';
import { createOpenRouter } from '@openrouter/ai-sdk-provider';

async function main() {
  await init({ apiKey: process.env.NEATLOGS_API_KEY, workflowName: 'my-agent' });
  const { generateText, tool } = wrapAISDK(ai);           // all LLM calls traced

  const openrouter = createOpenRouter({ apiKey: process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY });

  const runAgent = span({ kind: 'WORKFLOW' }, async (goal: string) => {
    return generateText({
      model: openrouter('deepseek/deepseek-chat'),
      // With a Vercel AI Gateway key instead: model: 'deepseek/deepseek-v3'
      prompt: goal,
      tools: { /* your tools; wrap their execute fns with span({kind:'TOOL'}) */ },
      stopWhen: ai.stepCountIs(10),                     // multi-step agent loop
    });
  });

  try { console.log((await runAgent('your task')).text); }
  finally { await flush(); await shutdown(); }            // otherwise traces get lost
}
main();
```

## Which key to use
- **Vercel AI Gateway key:** the simplest choice with the AI SDK. No extra provider package is needed; you just pass a `'provider/model'` string.
- **OpenRouter key:** the most model choice, using `@openrouter/ai-sdk-provider`.
- **DeepSeek key:** the cheapest, using `@ai-sdk/deepseek`. It only gives you DeepSeek models.

They all trace the same way in neatlogs. The model names in the example are placeholders, so check the exact IDs on your provider's model list.

## Two things to watch
1. Always `await flush()` and `await shutdown()` at the end of a script. The neatlogs docs say that skipping this is the most common reason traces don't appear.
2. Put **one `WORKFLOW` span around each run**. Without it, every LLM call shows up as a separate trace instead of one tree.

To scaffold the TypeScript project with this already wired in, tell me which key you'll use and what the agent should do.

Yes, you can build it on WhatsApp. Chat SDK has an **official WhatsApp adapter** (`@chat-adapter/whatsapp`) that uses Meta's WhatsApp Business Cloud API. It plugs into Mastra the same way as the other platforms.

## Architecture

```
User's WhatsApp ──► Meta Cloud API ──webhook──► your server (/api/webhooks/whatsapp)
                                                   │  Chat SDK + @chat-adapter/whatsapp
                                                   ▼
                                    identify({sessionId: thread.id}) ──► Mastra agent (wrapMastra)
                                                   │                        tools, memory, steps
                                                   ▼                              │
                                        thread.post(reply) ◄──────────────────────┘
                                                              all traced in neatlogs
```

## What you need from Meta (free, about 20 minutes)
1. Go to **developers.facebook.com/apps**, create a **Business** app and add the **WhatsApp** product.
2. Meta gives you a **free test phone number**, so you don't need your own business number. In development mode you can message up to 5 verified numbers; add your own phone as one of them.
3. Copy these four values into your `.env`:

| Variable | Where to find it |
|---|---|
| `WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN` | WhatsApp → API Setup. ⚠️ This token **expires after 24 hours**. For the full 48-hour hackathon, create a permanent **System User Token** |
| `WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID` | WhatsApp → API Setup |
| `WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET` | App Settings → Basic |
| `WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN` | Any random string you choose |

4. **The webhook needs a public HTTPS URL.** Locally, use `cloudflared tunnel` or `ngrok`, or deploy to Vercel. Then, in **WhatsApp → Configuration**:
   - Set the URL to `https://…/api/webhooks/whatsapp`.
   - Set the verify token to the same random string.
   - Subscribe to `messages` and `user_id_update`.

## The code in outline

```ts
// lib/bot.ts
import { Chat } from 'chat';
import { createWhatsAppAdapter } from '@chat-adapter/whatsapp';
import { createMemoryState } from '@chat-adapter/state-memory';
import { identify } from 'neatlogs';
import { agent } from './agent';          // wrapMastra(new Agent({...}))

export const bot = new Chat({
  userName: 'mybot',
  adapters: { whatsapp: createWhatsAppAdapter() },
  state: createMemoryState(),             // use Redis/Postgres for anything beyond local dev
});

bot.onDirectMessage(async (thread, message) => {
  await thread.markAsRead();
  await thread.startTyping();
  const reply = await identify(
    { sessionId: thread.id, endUserId: thread.id },    // one neatlogs session per WhatsApp chat
    async () => (await agent.generate(message.text)).text,
  );
  await thread.post({ markdown: reply });
});
```

## WhatsApp limits that affect the design
- **Streaming is buffered.** The adapter sends the finished reply, not a token-by-token stream, so use `agent.generate()`.
- **The 24-hour rule.** You can only reply freely within 24 hours of the user's last message. To message first, you need Meta-approved template messages.
- **Long tasks.** Meta's webhook expects a fast reply, so return immediately (`waitUntil`), post "working on it…", and send the result when it's done. This is also a natural way to show "complete a substantial task end to end" in your demo.
- **Formatting.** Messages over 4,096 characters are split automatically. Cards support at most 3 reply buttons, and files (PDF, images, audio) can be sent.

## Packages
```bash
npm i @mastra/core neatlogs chat @chat-adapter/whatsapp @chat-adapter/state-memory zod
npm i @openrouter/ai-sdk-provider   # or @ai-sdk/deepseek, depending on your key
```

